\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Prediction error on the \textbf  {held-out test set} (\SI {14830}{} samples), reported relative to the torque limit of each joint. The normalisation is necessary: in absolute value, the wrist joints (limit \SI {12}{\newton \meter }) are unreadable next to the load-bearing joints (\SI {87}{\newton \meter }). Two readings stand out. First, the analytical model is at its worst on the wrist (joints~5 and~6), which is therefore where the learned model has the most to contribute. Second, that is precisely where the grey-box model stays far from the black box, whereas it matches it on joints~1 to~4: the overall gap of \cref  {tab-baselines} is almost entirely a wrist gap. The remarks of \cref  {sec-res-partition} on the data partition apply to all three series.}}{12}{figure.caption.27}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig-perjoint-baselines}{{2}{12}{Prediction error on the \textbf {held-out test set} (\SI {14830}{} samples), reported relative to the torque limit of each joint. The normalisation is necessary: in absolute value, the wrist joints (limit \SI {12}{\newton \meter }) are unreadable next to the load-bearing joints (\SI {87}{\newton \meter }). Two readings stand out. First, the analytical model is at its worst on the wrist (joints~5 and~6), which is therefore where the learned model has the most to contribute. Second, that is precisely where the grey-box model stays far from the black box, whereas it matches it on joints~1 to~4: the overall gap of \cref {tab-baselines} is almost entirely a wrist gap. The remarks of \cref {sec-res-partition} on the data partition apply to all three series}{figure.caption.27}{}}
\newlabel{fig-perjoint-baselines@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][11][]12}}
